## E2 (L4) — The tournament: bias vs inference, four worlds

Same regression y = 0.0001 + 0.9·m + ε in four worlds (n = 1200,
β = 0.9, 600 runs each):

(a) **GM world** — ε ~ N(0, 0.01²): baseline.
(b) **Heteroskedastic world** — σ_t ∈ {0.005, 0.02} alternating by a
    slow regime process (or: ε_t ~ N(0, 0.01²·(0.5+2·|sin(t/80)|))):
    conditional variance varies, mean still 0.
(c) **Autocorrelated world** — ε_t = 0.6·ε_{t−1} + u_t (AR(1)).
(d) **Omitted-variable world** — y = 0.0001 + 0.9·m + 0.5·h + ε with
    h correlated ρ=0.7 with m, and you regress y on m ONLY.

For each world report: E[β̂] (bias?), sd of β̂ across runs, mean of
the classical per-fit SE, and the rejection rate of |t| > 1.96
testing β = 0.9 (the TRUE value — nominal 5%). Assemble the 4×4
table. Which worlds break unbiasedness, which break only inference?

